### B13 tab audio: early, thin, and noisy [light-medium · Robert's ear 2026-09-02 · INSTRUMENT BUILT, EAR HALF OWED]
Robert on rec_gpsoujs2sydf (124.8 min, prod, camera + mic + screen + tab audio): "only sound is not perfect, less bass or whatever, tab audio part of second faster than screen video i think, some small noises in tab audio". Three complaints. NO FIX HAS SHIPPED, no default has moved, and none of the three is a behaviour Robert has approved changing.
BUILT 2026-09-02 (instrumentation and one lever, both proven end-to-end through the app on a local build of this branch):
- THE TAKE NOW SAYS WHAT THE PLATFORM DELIVERED. `ChannelDiagnostics.audioTrack` carries ec / ns / agc / channelCount / sampleRate / latencyMs for every measured audio channel, read at capture start AND again at stop (`atStop` appears only when something moved mid-take, which is crbug 344876285's signature). `null` is not `false` — "the platform reported nothing" is the finding on a display-audio track and a boolean cannot hold both. Measured landing in a real take: `{"echoCancellation":false,"noiseSuppression":false,"autoGainControl":false,"channelCount":2,"sampleRate":44100,"latencyMs":10}`. This closes B13's first gate and G6(h)'s tab-audio half; G6(h) still owns `audiotap`.
- THE LEVER: `?looplat=0` (docs/FLAGS.md). Stops the input-latency subtraction on LOOPBACK channels only; a mic keeps it under every setting. DEFAULT UNCHANGED — subtract everywhere, exactly as shipped, pinned by test.
- THE TAKE SAYS WHICH WAY IT WAS MADE. `anchor.reportedInputLatencyMs` now means "what the platform said" and the new `anchor.inputLatencyApplied` says whether it was used. The first cut shipped without the second field reaching the take — session.ts rebuilds the anchor field by field — and the measurement run read `null` on both variants; pinned by test now.
- WHAT THE LEVER IS WORTH, isolated from the picture's own spin-up: `latencyContributionMs` = (where the sound was PLACED against the picture) − (where the two channels actually ARRIVED). Shipped path **+9.9 / +10.6 ms of lead** across runs; `?looplat=0` **+0.3 ms**. Comparing the whole lead across two takes would be comparing two device spin-ups; this compares the decision.
- ONE SUSPECT ELIMINATED WITHOUT ANY CAPTURE: the opus floor. The reference 12-tone signal through `AudioEncoder`/`AudioDecoder` at opus 128 kbps stereo comes back within **0.3 dB at every octave from 31.25 Hz to 16 kHz**. The codec is not "less bass".
- X14a SAYS THE MECHANISM IS NOT ONLY THE 10 ms. The read-time audio anchor is 12.9 ms LATE of true capture on a real mic (batch 10 ms + transport 3 ms). So the 10 ms subtraction is roughly cancelling a real pipeline lateness rather than inventing one, and B13's opening theory — "a loopback has no input latency, so subtracting it is simply wrong" — is only right if the loopback's pipeline delay is much smaller than a mic's. THAT IS THE NEXT MEASUREMENT, and it is one line of the same probe on a machine that can open a display source.
GATES NOT MET, and why: **no real tab-audio loopback is reachable from an agent-launched Chrome here.** Every getDisplayMedia rung returns `NotReadableError` (see X14a for the full list of combinations tried), and `--use-fake-device-for-media-stream` substitutes a GENERATOR, not the tab's loopback — measured: a 1234 Hz tone played in the page did not appear in the captured track (page tone −57.9 dB, the generator's own content −52.6 dB). So the spectrum, the mono check, the 46.4 ms lead on a fresh take and the A/B files all wait on Robert's Chrome. The rig refuses to print a spectrum verdict when its own marker tone is missing, so a wrong-surface run cannot be mistaken for a finding.
ONE COMMAND ON HIS MACHINE: `node scripts/b13-tabaudio.mjs --looplat=both --takeMs=45000` — records both ways, writes `~/Downloads/inout-b13/b13-shipped.mp4` and `b13-looplat0.mp4` for his ear, and prints the four spectra with the bass delta per octave.
