- `metadata().captureTime` DOES NOT EXIST HERE. `VideoFrame.metadata()` is a function and returns `{}` — zero keys — on both an I420 display frame and an NV12 camera frame. `AudioData` has no `metadata()` at all (`typeof === 'undefined'`). Idea 4a's literal field is empty on every source; nothing can be built on it.
- `AudioData.timestamp` IS a capture stamp under another name, and it is USABLE. It sits on the `performance.now()` timeline DIRECTLY (no epoch conversion), and read-time minus stamp over 120 s on a real mic = **12.91 ms, sd 0.86, drift −0.37 ms/min** (three runs: 11.66 / 13.47 / 12.91, sd 0.6–1.7). The batch is 480 frames = 10.00 ms, so that 12.9 ms is `10 ms of batch + ~3 ms of transport` — it is the lateness the shipped read-time anchor bakes in, and reading the stamp instead of the arrival removes it. sd 0.86 ms is well under G5's 6.5 ms per-run term, so this is worth taking.
- THE SAME MEASUREMENT ON SUBSTITUTED DEVICES READS 0.74 ms. That is the B7 lesson again in one line: a fake source has no capture pipeline, so a synthetic rig would have reported this anchor as already perfect.
- `VideoFrame.timestamp` is present and sits on the SYSTEM MONOTONIC clock, not `performance.now()` — a constant offset (−219,016,417 ms in one run) held to sd 0.80 ms. A constant offset is a calibration, not a disqualification; the first cut of this probe's verdict rule called it "drift" and the measurement corrected it.
